Before substantive work, my notes:

**1. What is the system ultimately trying to become better at learning?**
Choosing what is worth learning, learning it efficiently, falsifying/validating it independently, preserving the evidence, and selecting the next frontier.

**2. What currently most constrains useful learning?**
The momentum edge is admitted as candidate positive evidence but never tested under transaction costs — cost robustness is the main remaining qualification.

**3. What uncertainty prevents a better decision?**
Whether the mechanical daily-rebalanced momentum edge survives realistic costs (it trades almost every bar, so cost drag could plausibly erase 0.05-0.13 median log returns).

**4. Single bounded action most likely to reduce it?**
Run a fixed, a-priori cost grid (slippage/commission levels) on lookback 3/5/10 momentum across the 10-asset universe through the existing walk-forward + matched-null + regime-stability gate, then independently verify.

**5. Why worth its effort/complexity/risk?**
LOW complexity (existing framework + cost knobs already in `BacktestConfig`), LOW execution risk (parameter sweep only), HIGH falsification power — it directly decides whether the one positive candidate is decision-relevant.

