Confirmed. This is a **genuine, important methodological finding**: the engine's symmetric proportional slippage (always added to fill price regardless of side) is **structurally neutral-to-beneficial for a continuously-invested long/short strategy** that flips direction, because the closing/selling leg receives the inflated fill price. This is a cost-model artifact, not an exploitable edge.

This means my initial cost grid (which used proportional slippage via `slippage_cents`) does NOT actually stress-test the momentum edge meaningfully — the fixed commission component does bite (harsh_fixed drag -0.0447 on AMZN), but the slippage component cancels.

